---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Veridian Contraption | Ray Weiss"
  description="Veridian Contraption — an autonomous ASCII world-simulator of considerable density and dubious intent. Playable web demo; released on itch under Conflict Simulations LLC."
>
  <main class="doc">
    <div class="doc__frame">
      <header class="page-heading">
        <nav class="eyebrow" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <a href="/conflict-simulations-llc"
            >Conflict Simulations LLC</a
          >
        </nav>
        <h1>Veridian Contraption</h1>
        <p class="dek">A World-Simulator of Considerable Density and Dubious Intent.</p>
      </header>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          The gates of Weneld-Uck admitted Ist-Wedilk in the usual manner, which is to say: with
          neither welcome nor objection.
        </p>
        <footer>
          In-game annals &middot; tick 14,802
        </footer>
      </blockquote>

      <div class="prose-body">
        <p>
          An autonomous ASCII world-simulator. Settlements glow amber. Caravans crawl along trade
          routes. Forgotten beasts patrol in magenta. Levies march in blood-red. The narrative log
          reads like a bureaucrat who has absorbed too much Borges.
        </p>
        <p>
          Civilizations rise and decline. Factions schism. Wars start over insults. Cults emerge,
          persecute, escalate, or fizzle. Eschatons arrive and reshape the map without ending the
          simulation. The world is doing something on its own, and you are watching it happen — come
          back later and there is always more to read.
        </p>
        <p>
          Built for the audience that liked Dwarf Fortress's narrative layer more than the colony
          management. Players who thought <em>Dune</em>'s appendices were the best part. You are
          spectator, occasional nudge, curator of emergent absurdity.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose-body terminal-section">
        <h2>Play it in your browser</h2>
        <p>
          The genuine simulation engine, compiled to WebAssembly — the same Rust worldgen, 196+
          event types, and procedural bureaucratic prose as the full game. It runs autonomously: the
          world proceeds whether you watch or not. It opens fullscreen in a new tab —
          <kbd>Space</kbd> to pause,
          <kbd>L</kbd> for the map legend,
          <kbd>?</kbd> for help.
        </p>
      </div>

      <a
        href="/veridian-contraption-demo/index.html"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener" class="download-tile"
      >
        <span class="download-tile__title"
          >&#9658;&nbsp; Play the web demo</span
        >
        <span class="download-tile__meta"
          >opens fullscreen, in a new tab</span
        >
      </a>

      <div>
        <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable table" tabindex="0">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th
                  >System</th
                >
                <th
                  >What it does</th
                >
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Simulation core</td>
                <td>
                  196+ event types in five prose registers (Clinical, Lyrical, Bureaucratic,
                  Ominous, Conspiratorial). 21 quest types pursued autonomously by agents.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Patron actions</td>
                <td>
                  Sponsor artists. Introduce rumors. Declare embargoes. Found guilds, cults, and
                  mercenary companies. Grant titles.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Military</td>
                <td>
                  Levies, cavalry, artillery. CRT combat resolved with tabletop-inspired tables.
                  Sieges with organic wall development. Multi-phase off-map invasions with
                  three-tier foreshadowing.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Magic</td>
                <td>
                  Six traditions: Necromancer, Elementalist, Geomancer, Bureaucromancer, Cleric,
                  Illusionist. Wizard towers, corruption, rogue wizards.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Politics</td>
                <td>
                  Coups, purges, show trials, espionage, demagogues, ideological spread.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Anomalies</td>
                <td>
                  Sixteen rare localized event types. R'lyeh Deep One Emergence with foreshadowing,
                  surfacing, and diplomatic Deep Ones. Seven Eschaton catastrophe classes that
                  reshape the world without ending the simulation.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Trade and ecology</td>
                <td>
                  27 trade goods. A* caravan pathfinding. Naval blockades during sieges. 24 animal
                  species with predator-prey dynamics and seasonal cycles.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Artist system</td>
                <td>
                  Poets, bards, and visual artists create feeling-driven works. Great works enter
                  the World Annals.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>World presets</td>
                <td>
                  Five flavors: The Long Bureaucracy, The Burning Provinces, The Deep Taxonomy, The
                  Conspiratorial Age, Unguided.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Cross-world</td>
                <td>
                  The Atlas screen tracks entities and expeditions across multiple saved worlds. The
                  Bureau of Absent Persons keeps records.
                </td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose-body terminal-section">
        <h2>Soundtrack</h2>
        <p>
          Fourteen original tracks, written, performed, and recorded by Ray Weiss. The title piece, <em
            >Movement 2</em
          >, opens the game and sits in a shuffle-without-replacement pool — it recurs about once a
          cycle, like any other track. Normalized to &minus;16 LUFS and mixed low: the music sits
          under whatever else you are doing.
        </p>
        <pre class="doc-glyphs">&#9601;&#9602;&#9603;&#9605;&#9606;&#9607;&#9608;&#9607;&#9606;&#9605;&#9603;&#9604;&#9606;&#9607;&#9608;&#9607;&#9606;&#9604;&#9603;&#9602;&#9601;&#9602;&#9603;&#9605;&#9606;&#9607;&#9606;&#9605;&#9604;&#9603;&#9602;&#9601;&#9602;&#9603;&#9604;&#9605;&#9606;&#9607;&#9608;&#9607;&#9606;&#9605;&#9604;&#9603;&#9602;&#9601;</pre>
        <p>
          The composition matches the register the simulation has been gesturing at: slow accretion,
          dense detail, occasional bursts of weirdness, the sense that the world is doing something
          on its own.
        </p>

        <h2>Stack</h2>
        <p>
          Rust 2021, a terminal with truecolor support. Variable world size from Small to Huge,
          scrollable viewport and minimap. Byte-identical save/load — resume from any saved tick and
          the world history is bit-for-bit identical — backed by a 25,000-tick determinism gate in
          the test suite. Multiple named worlds, autosave, eleven document report types, TXT history
          export.
        </p>

        <h2>Lineage</h2>
        <p>
          The aesthetic: Dwarf Fortress, Caves of Qud, <em>The Illuminatus! Trilogy</em>, early
          D&amp;D modules, Nabokov. Read into the design: Engels, Dupuy, Clausewitz, Kafka, the <em
            >Principia Discordia</em
          >, Norman Cohn, Thomas M&uuml;ntzer, Lovecraft, and Shea &amp; Wilson.
        </p>

        <h2>Buy</h2>
        <p>
          On itch under Conflict Simulations LLC at $3 — <a
            href="https://conflict-simulations-llc.itch.io/veridian-contraption"
            >conflict-simulations-llc.itch.io/veridian-contraption</a
          >. Buy in now and you are done paying: when Veridian Contraption debuts on Steam at $9.99,
          existing itch owners get free Steam keys, no repurchase. The itch price rises to $9.99 in
          lockstep with the Steam launch — but anyone who bought early stays at the price they paid.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="doc-coda">
        If you played along through Early Access — thank you. The bug reports, the world
        screenshots, and the occasional message about a strange Eschaton playthrough turned this
        into a real release rather than a research project.<br />&mdash; Ray
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
